 "near_miss": {
  "formula": "A_ig_lqd_usig_z63c",
  "why": "LQD vs USIG, two IG corporate ETFs on near-identical portfolios; the only formula stable across the split and positive on BOTH windows at the arithmetic tick-floor bound. NOT REAL: its open-to-open gross (0.79 design / 0.71 held out) is below the bar (0.87 / 1.17) on both windows, so it fails on gross before cost is discussed.",
  "cost_over_gross": 1.1061191945324291
 },
 "findings": [
  "The published-CURVE tethers fail on the CLOCK: rho1(dspread) = -0.465 (Treasury H.15) and -0.383 (TIPS breakeven), because FRED publishes a 15:30 ET snapshot while the ETF prints at 16:00. Mechanism independently established by INVENT_0823_rates (their -0.422 vs my -0.465). The TIPS-breakeven anchor is a FRESH instance of the same failure.",
  "The SIBLING tether has NO clock problem (both legs are ETFs struck at 16:00, rho1 -0.20 to -0.26) and still dies: open-to-open gross of 1.95 CLEARS every null bar (0.87), so the tether is genuinely informative, but net is -2.84 because cost runs 2.4x gross. The mispricing is 0.14-0.21 bp/day against a 0.6-1.25 bp half-spread.",
  "Programme thesis gains a third clause: a published tether must be SYNCHRONOUS *and* its deviation must EXCEED THE SPREAD. Bond-ETF siblings satisfy the first and fail the second.",
  "Borrow is NOT the binding constraint here -- every leg is a bond ETF, easiest-to-borrow, and borrow costs < 0.02 bp/day. This family dies purely on spread.",
  "Daily bond-ETF NAV history is the concrete paid-data ask: the tether value IS published daily by every issuer and is NOT machine-readable free."
 ],
